    "body": "EICMA 2026 • 2026 EICMA's Speed Legends Exhibit Was Not To Be Missed – See the Bikes That Paved MotoGP History\nEREVs May Dodge EU Hybrid Caps but Still Face BEV Tariffs\n19 photos\nPhoto: Leapmotor\nThe European Union's planned restrictions on hybrid vehicle imports may create an opening for Chinese automakers in particular. Electric vehicles equipped with range-extended powertrains may avoid the proposed limits, even as plug-in hybrids and conventional hybrids face tighter access into the EU. There is, however, a catch that has to be highlighted.\nAccording to Nikkei, extended-range electric vehicles could remain subject to the European bloc's existing anti-subsidy duties on Chinese battery-electric vehicles. In other words, Chinese manufacturers would be left with a choice between potentially higher import costs and restricted sales.\nBefore going any further, a plug-in hybrid is a different animal from an extended-range electric vehicle because PHEVs can provide mechanical propulsion. EREVs, meanwhile, use their internal combustion engines to supply electricity to the electric drive system and the traction battery, with propulsion handled exclusively by the electric motor(s) even with a low state of charge.\nA seemingly narrow distinction, for sure. The European Commission's existing customs treatment places EREVs in the BEV category instead of grouping them with hybrids. Naturally, this means that extended-range electric vehicles fall outside the classifications covered by the proposed hybrid import restrictions.\nSaid classification traces its roots back to Regulation 2024/2754, which classified these vehicles under customs code 8703 80 10 as battery-electric vehicles. The reasoning? An engine that only generates electricity doesn't make the vehicle a conventional hybrid in the relevant customs classification.\nPhoto: Leapmotor Hybrids and plug-in hybrids are listed under customs codes 8703 40 10, 8703 50 00, 8703 60 10, and 8703 70 00, depending on configuration. Given the aforementioned, EREVs may escape the proposed annual import ceiling for hybrids without escaping the current BEV penalties.\nChinese battery-electric vehicles face European countervailing duties of up to 35.3 percent, depending on the automaker, plus the bloc's 10 percent import duty. These duties would continue to apply to qualifying EREVs imported from the People's Republic of China, which is the largest market for new passenger vehicles in terms of domestic sales. Around 24 million were sold there in 2025 alone.\nNikkei underlines that pricing and profitability would still be a problem with Chinese manufacturers, which may pass part of the costs to customers. In this case, EREVs would become less competitive in the European market. On the other hand, Chinese automakers could very well agree to less money on each imported EREV as long as they create a niche for themselves in the European space.\nLeapmotor, which produces the Euro-market B10 crossover at the Stellantis Zaragoza plant in Spain, offers an EREV option. Marketed as the REEV Hybrid in the Spanish market, said model uses a rear-mounted electric motor that delivers 160 kW (215 hp) and 240 Nm (177 lb-ft) of twist. Up front, a 1.5-liter naturally aspirated I4 powers the onboard generator, rated at 50 kW (67 hp) by its maker.\nA lithium-iron-phosphate battery with 18.8 kWh also needs mentioning, with Leapmotor Espana promising up to 86 kilometers (53 miles) of electric driving range. In the WLTP cycle, total combined range is estimated at 900 kilometers (559 miles) with a full charge and a full tank of dinosaur juice.\nVia // Nikkei\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nEREV PHEV Hybrid Europe China\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT NASA Turns to Private Companies to Develop Next-Gen Hall Effect Thrusters for Spacecraft",
